# makepkg cannot source a PKGBUILD with CRLF, and it validates every vendored
# source file (gdbinit + the .patch stack) against pinned sha256sums — a Windows
# checkout (core.autocrlf) rewrites them to CRLF, changing their bytes so the
# integrity check fails. Keep the whole vendored GCC recipe LF on every platform.
Tools/gcc-toolchain/recipe/** text eol=lf
Tools/gcc-toolchain/recipe/PKGBUILD text eol=lf

# The same hazard one directory over, and it bites harder: build_gcc_wasm.py's
# apply_patches keys its stamp on a patch's bytes and refuses to re-apply a changed one,
# naming "delete that source tree" as the fix. A Windows checkout rewriting these to CRLF
# changes every digest, so the script demands a multi-hour toolchain rebuild for a patch
# series that is in fact already applied -- and a CRLF patch against GCC's LF sources is
# a rejected hunk waiting to happen on a clean clone.
Tools/gcc-toolchain/wasm/** text eol=lf

# CMake's file(STRINGS) keeps a trailing carriage return, so a CRLF checkout of this list
# yields paths that fail EXISTS. Both readers strip their lines, which makes this a pin
# rather than the fix -- but a data file two build systems parse has no reason to carry
# platform line endings.
Engine/cmake/native_gpu_backend_units.txt text eol=lf
Engine/cmake/platform_impl_arms.txt text eol=lf
Engine/cmake/gpu_profile_backend_arms.txt text eol=lf
Engine/cmake/excluded_impl_units.txt text eol=lf
